<!-- livebook:{"break_markdown":true} -->

*You are here: the enterprise floor. Everything the desk did so far was behavior; from this lesson on it is also record.*

A row tells you what is true now. An enterprise also has to answer *what happened, who did it, and when*: for audit, for debugging (lesson 14's step 4), and as the input every later layer consumes. Projections (16), compliance (18) and process triggers (21) all read **the event log**, not the tables.

`ash_events` records every action on an audited resource as an event row, in the same transaction as the write. In `ash_enterprise` you do not add it per resource: **batteries are inherited.** `AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource` adds `AshEvents.Events` pointing at the one central log, `AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog`, unless a resource opts out with `audit?: false`, a local, greppable decision.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> A write and its record now land in the same breath. Systems that learn this late pay years of archaeology for it.</p>
</div>

## The tutorial domain and the Refund

Create these two files.

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial do
  use Ash.Domain, otp_app: :ash_enterprise

  resources do
    resource AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund do
      define :propose_refund, action: :propose, args: [:ticket_ref, :amount]
      define :approve_refund, action: :approve
      define :reject_refund, action: :reject
    end
  end
end
```

<!-- livebook:{"force_markdown":true} -->

```elixir
# lib/ash_enterprise/tutorial/refund.ex
defmodule AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund do
  use AshEnterprise.Platform.Resource,
    domain: AshEnterprise.Tutorial,
    ownership: :organization_owned,
    lifecycle?: false

  postgres do
    table "tutorial_refunds"
    repo AshEnterprise.Repo
  end

  attributes do
    uuid_primary_key :id

    attribute :ticket_ref, :string, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
    attribute :amount, :decimal, allow_nil?: false, public?: true, constraints: [greater_than: 0]
    attribute :reason, :string, public?: true
    attribute :status, :atom,
      constraints: [one_of: [:proposed, :approved, :rejected]],
      default: :proposed, allow_nil?: false, public?: true
  end

  actions do
    defaults [:read]

    create :propose do
      accept [:ticket_ref, :amount, :reason]
    end

    update :approve do
      validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
      change set_attribute(:status, :approved)
    end

    update :reject do
      validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
      change set_attribute(:status, :rejected)
    end
  end
end
```

This is the most setup any lesson will ask of you — everything after this rides on the fabric you build here. No asides while we do it; plumbing deserves silence.

Five steps, and the order matters. (1) Create the files above. (2) Add `AshEnterprise.Tutorial` to `config :ash_enterprise, ash_domains: [...]` in `config/config.exs` — the codegen task discovers domains through that list. (3) In a normal terminal (not the attached IEx; these are mix tasks and boot their own node), generate the migration and apply it:

```bash
devenv shell -- mix ash.codegen --dev
devenv shell -- mix ash.migrate
```

4. Right after, refresh the **privilege catalogue** so your new resource's actions are grantable at all:

```bash
devenv shell -- mix ash_enterprise.seed --privileges-only
```

   (Eight new privileges, one per `Tutorial.Refund` verb. Run this after *every* codegen that adds actions or resources — lesson 16 adds one too.)

5. Finally **restart the app node** (Ctrl-C twice, then the overview's start command) and reconnect Livebook's runtime, re-running the actor/tenant cell below once you are reattached. A restart is the only way new resource or domain modules load with their extension state: `recompile()` compiles the file but the platform's injected sections stay stale until boot. Application env — like that new `ash_domains` entry — is also read at boot.

Now look at what you wrote versus what you got. The `uuid_primary_key :id` is yours — every platform resource declares its own (see `lib/ash_enterprise/accounts/organization.ex`); the platform injects timestamps (`created_on`/`modified_on`), provenance (`created_by_id` and friends), `organization_id` with attribute multitenancy, archival, the policy set and the `events do` block, but **never your primary key**. Ask your agent (lesson 14) where the rest came from:

```elixir
AshAgentTools.describe_resource(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund)
|> get_in([:fields])
|> Enum.map(& &1.name)
```

## Set up your actor and tenant

Nothing in `mix setup` seeds a sign-in-able user, and you should not need one from the demo seeder: build the small fabric every lesson from here on assumes. This cell is **idempotent** — find-or-create at every step, so re-running it after a restart is a no-op. It mirrors what `AshEnterprise.Platform.Seeder.seed_tenant/1` does for a real tenant, through the same actions:

```elixir
require Ash.Query

alias AshEnterprise.Accounts
alias AshEnterprise.Security
alias AshEnterprise.Platform.SystemActor

# Seeding is the one operation no role can authorize (the role model lives
# inside the tenant being created), so bootstrap as the platform's named
# seed actor. Every write is still attributed to it in the event log.
boot = SystemActor.seed()

organization =
  Accounts.Organization
  |> Ash.Query.filter(unique_name == "tutorial")
  |> Ash.read_one!(authorize?: false) ||
    Ash.create!(
      Ash.Changeset.for_create(Accounts.Organization, :create, %{
        name: "Tutorial Corp",
        unique_name: "tutorial"
      }, actor: boot)
    )

tenant = organization.id

root =
  Accounts.BusinessUnit
  |> Ash.Query.filter(name == "Tutorial Desk")
  |> Ash.read_one!(actor: boot, tenant: tenant) ||
    Ash.create!(
      Ash.Changeset.for_create(Accounts.BusinessUnit, :create, %{name: "Tutorial Desk"},
        actor: boot,
        tenant: tenant
      )
    )

role =
  Security.Role
  |> Ash.Query.filter(name == "Administrator")
  |> Ash.read_one!(actor: boot, tenant: tenant) ||
    Ash.create!(
      Ash.Changeset.for_create(Security.Role, :create, %{
        name: "Administrator",
        owning_business_unit_id: root.id
      }, actor: boot, tenant: tenant)
    )

email = "you@tutorial.example"

user =
  Accounts.User
  |> Ash.Query.filter(email == ^email)
  |> Ash.read_one!(authorize?: false) ||
    Ash.create!(
      Ash.Changeset.for_create(Accounts.User, :register_with_password, %{
        email: email,
        password: "tutorial1234",
        password_confirmation: "tutorial1234"
      }, authorize?: false)
    )

# The scoping business unit is required explicitly: the default derives it
# from the *acting* user's unit, and the seed actor has none.
Security.UserRole.assign!(
  %{user_id: user.id, role_id: role.id, scoping_business_unit_id: root.id},
  actor: boot,
  tenant: tenant
)

actor = user
:ok
```

Note what the User does **not** have: an `organization_id` attribute. Tenancy is a column on the *resources*, not on people — your tenant is `organization.id`, carried separately. That is why the cell binds `tenant` itself.

Your role exists but grants nothing yet: the Administrator role links privileges at provisioning time, and your `Tutorial.Refund` privileges only came into existence with the codegen. Grant everything grantable the same way the seeder does:

```elixir
AshEnterprise.Platform.Seeder.regrant_administrator_privileges(tenant)
```

Re-run this after every future codegen that adds resources or actions (lesson 16 will need it again). If a call still comes back forbidden, that is lesson 14: `AshAgentTools.can(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :propose, actor)` and `explain_forbidden/2` tell you which inherited policy said no.

## Write, then read the log

Propose a refund of `25.00` for ticket `"T-1001"`, then approve it.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  opts = [actor: actor, tenant: tenant]

  refund = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.propose_refund!("T-1001", Decimal.new("25.00"), opts)
  refund = AshEnterprise.Tutorial.approve_refund!(refund, opts)
  ```

  If this is forbidden, that is lesson 14: `AshAgentTools.can(AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund, :propose, actor)` and `explain_forbidden/2` tell you which inherited policy said no — most likely a privilege your role has not been granted yet (the `regrant` call above).

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** Two rows in the log — `:propose` then `:approve` — one business act per named action, with an actor and a correlation id attached.

**Look around.** Re-run the read cell above and read its output as a sentence: *proposed, then approved.* Every later layer in this series reads that same sentence — projections count it, rules judge it, processes start from it. You have just written the desk's first paragraph.

Now read the events for that record. The event log is a resource, so you query it with Ash. (This cell fetches the refund itself, so it works even on a fresh run before you have written the exercise solution above):

```elixir
require Ash.Query

refund =
  AshEnterprise.Tutorial.Refund
  |> Ash.Query.filter(ticket_ref == "T-1001")
  |> Ash.Query.sort(created_on: :desc)
  |> Ash.Query.limit(1)
  |> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
  |> List.first()

AshEnterprise.Audit.EventLog
|> Ash.Query.filter(record_id == ^refund.id)
|> Ash.Query.sort(occurred_at: :asc)
|> Ash.read!(actor: actor, tenant: tenant)
|> Enum.map(&{&1.action, &1.action_type, &1.data})
```

Two events: `:propose` with the accepted inputs, `:approve` with the change. Each also carries the actor, the tenant and `metadata["correlation_id"]`, which ties together all the events one request produced.

## The rules of the log

* **Only actions write events.** That is why lesson 11 insisted actions are the only door. An `Ecto` update around the action leaves no event, and every later layer will be wrong without knowing it.
* **The log is append-only and tamper-evident.** `ash_enterprise` hash-chains it per tenant (ADR 0020), riding on the advisory lock `ash_events` already takes.
* **Replay re-runs actions.** Changes still execute during replay. So nothing non-deterministic belongs in a `change`: no HTTP, no `DateTime.utc_now/0` you did not capture as input, and never a model call (you will see this as a law in lesson 22). Put remote calls in generic actions (lesson 13) whose *results* are recorded as inputs.

<div class="rounded-xl p-4 my-4" style="background-color: #fdf6ec; border-left: 4px solid #c2410c; color: #7c2d12;">
  <p><i class="ri-fire-fill"></i> <strong>Soot was here.</strong> Append-only suits a salamander: nothing I saw was ever unsaid. The hash chain means the log can make the same promise.</p>
</div>

## Exercise: name the event

Events are as meaningful as action names. Add `update :request_info` that accepts `:reason` and keeps status `:proposed`. Call it, then confirm the log says `request_info`, not `update`.

**Enter your solution**

```elixir

```

<details class="rounded-lg my-4" style="background-color: #96ef86; color: #040604;">
  <summary class="cursor-pointer font-bold p-4">Show Solution</summary>
  <div class="p-4">

  ```elixir
  update :request_info do
    accept [:reason]
    validate attribute_equals(:status, :proposed)
  end
  ```

  Add `define :request_refund_info, action: :request_info` to the domain, restart the node (step 5 — a DSL change), and call `AshEnterprise.Tutorial.request_refund_info!(refund, %{reason: "warranty"}, actor: actor, tenant: tenant)`. A generic `:update` with a status field would record *that something changed*; a named action records *what the business did*. Projections and process triggers key on these names.

  </div>
</details>

**Checkpoint.** The log now reads `request_info`, not `update` — a business act, named. Lessons 16 and 21 will key on exactly that name.

When you are finished with the whole tutorial, the overview's **When you're done** recipe rolls back this lesson's migration and reverts its config line and snapshots.

